<solution>
    <program>
        <input>
% Problem 2 in MATLAB
>> abs(pi-3)
ans =   0.141592653589793
>> abs(pi-3)/pi
ans =  0.045070341448628


%  Problem 6 part a)
 
>> abs(sqrt(2)-1.414)
ans =  2.135623730952219e-04
>> abs(sqrt(2)-1.414)/(sqrt(2))
ans = 1.510114022219229e-04

% part b)
>> abs(10^(pi)-1400)
ans =  14.544268632989315
>> abs(10^(pi)-1400)/(10^(pi))
ans =   0.010497822704619
% Problem 7
>> 1103*sqrt(8)/9801
ans =   0.318309878440470
%Problem 9
% part a) 
>> floor(log(234567))    
ans = 12
%part b) 
>> exp(ceil(log(234567)))
ans =  4.424133920089205e+05
%part f)
>> floor(cos(3)-nthroot(log(3),5))/ceil(atan(3)-exp(3))
ans =  0.166666666666667
% Problem 19
a)
>> x = 0:5;
>> sum(1./factorial(x))
ans =   2.716666666666666  
</input>
</program>

</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise number=" ">
    <title>Question 1.A</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Ideally, a numerical method converges toward the exact answer of mathematical problem. However, in reality there are several different issues that can arise. A lack of consistency could mean that the method converges, but not to the solution to the mathematical problem. Describe how a person can check to make sure the method they have is being consistent.
        </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
        <p>
            To check for consistency, one can perform a refinement study (such as a grid or step-size refinement) and verify that the local truncation error approaches zero as the step size (<m>h</m> or <m>\Delta t</m>) approaches zero. Alternatively, the method can be tested against an analytical solution with known properties to ensure the error scales at the theoretical order of accuracy.
        </p>
    </solution>
</exercise>

<exercise number=" ">
    <title>Question 1.B</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            The book and notes do reference the accumulation of error. Describe what can cause accumulation of error and what can be done to mitigate this issue.
