</exercise>

<exercise number=" ">
    <title>Question 1.E</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Are percentage error and absolute relative error the same thing?
        </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
        <p> The simple answer is that they are not the same. The percentage error does have the conversion factor of multiplying by 100. In addition, percentage error is not always defined to be non-negative (depending on your source for the definition) while absolute relative error is forced to be non-negative by definition. We will generally prefer the use of absolute relative error and we will seldom discuss the percentage error, which is likely a sharp contrast to what you will see in other classes.
            </p>
    </solution>
</exercise>

<exercise number=" ">
    <title>Question 1.F</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            The Brin book [LB16] and the class notes both reference big oh notation, which is mathematical notation to represent asymptotic convergence. This same concept is covered in the class notes in section 1.4. In MATH 373, the common use of big Oh is classify convergence as the step size, <m>h</m>, shrinks to zero. Prove that the second order Taylor's approximation of <m>f(x)=e^{x}</m> is <m>{\cal O}(h^{3})</m> where the center is 0 and <m>h=x-x_{0}</m>.
        </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
        <p>
            The analysis comes down to the remainder term <m>R_2(x) = \frac {f^{(3)}(x)(x-x_0)^3}{3!}</m>, this simplifies the error to be 
 <men>\frac {e^{\xi}}6 (x-x_0)^3 = Mh^3.</men>
 This follows from <m>h=x-x_0</m> and <m>\frac {e^{\xi}}6</m> is equal to some constant. Therefore, the approximation is <m>{\cal O}(h^3).</m>
            </p>
    </solution>
</exercise>

<exercise number=" ">
    <title>Question 1.G</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Consider a numerical method gives an estimate of <m>a_{h}</m>, which is an approximation to <m>a</m> using the step size of <m>h</m>. We are able to determine that
            <md>
                <mrow>\vert a-a_{h} \vert \le M(2h^{2} + 9h^{3}).</mrow>
            </md>
            What is the big oh estimate in this case?
        </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
        <p> The measurement of big oh is how fast the error goes to zero as h goes to zero. Hence, terms with h to a higher power will converge faster to zero and the only elements that will slow convergence are the terms in the error expression that have <m>h</m> to a lower power. Therefore, our order in this case is <m>{\cal O}(h^2)</m>.
            </p>
    </solution>
</exercise>

<exercise number=" ">
    <title>Question 1.H</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Given that <m>a_{h}</m> converges to <m>a</m> and the error of a calculation using step size <m>h</m> results in
            <md>
                <mrow>a-a_{h} = h^{3}-4h^{3/2}.</mrow>
            </md>
            What is the big Oh estimate in this case?
        </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
        <p> It is important to keep in mind that we are looking at absolute error so the real expression is <m> \vert a-a_h \vert \le \vert h^3-4h^{3/2}\vert </m>. As with the previous problem, the interest is to look at the lowest power to see what would converge the slowest as <m>h\rightarrow 0</m>, which in this case points to <m>{\cal O}(h^{3/2})</m>.
            </p>
    </solution>
</exercise>

<exercise number=" ">
    <title>Question 1.I</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Big oh also shows up in computer science, but the use is different. What is the main difference between the use of big oh in numerical analysis and how it is used in Computer Science?
        </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
        <p> The application in Computer Science is looking for asymptotic growth as <m>n \rightarrow \infty</m>, but in Numerical Analysis our concern is what is happening as step size gets smaller, i.e. <m>h \rightarrow 0</m>. Thus, the analysis in Computer Science will often look at the highest powers in the expression involving <m>n</m> since it is looking at growth, while Numerical Analysis has a concern of identifying the terms with the lowest powers of <m>h</m>. It turns out the two concepts enjoy a connection since we will often define <m>h = \frac {b-a}n</m> and so the growth of <m>n</m> can be tied to the shrinking of <m>h</m>.
            </p>
    </solution>
</exercise>

<exercise number=" ">
<title> <alert> Section 1.2</alert> out of [LB16] </title>
<statement>
 Problems: 2, 3, 4, 5, 7, 14, 16, 21, and 23 Use Matlab to solve problems: 1, 8, 10, and 26
</statement>
<solution>
<p>Problem 1 part g)</p>
<p> <m> f(x) = \cos^2(x);</m> <m> f'(x) = -2 \cos(x) \sin(x);</m> <m>f''(x) = 2 \sin^2(x) - 2 \cos^2(x);</m> and <m> f'''(x) = 8 \cos(x) \sin(x);</m> and <m> f^{(4)}(x)=8(\cos^2(x) - \sin^2(x)).</m> Thus, <m> T_3(x) = 1 - x^2</m> with <m>R_3(x) = f^{(4)}(\xi) \frac {x^4}{4!}</m> </p>

<p> Problem 7 <m>f(x) = \cosh(x)</m>, <m>R_{43}(x) = \frac {f^{(44)}(\xi) (x-x_0)^{44}}{44!}</m>, which for the given <m>f(x)</m> it follows that <m> R_{43}(x) = \frac {\cosh(\xi) (x-x_0)^{44}}{44!}</m></p>
<p> Problem 14 <m>f(x) = e^x \cos (x)</m>; <m> f'(x) = e^x \cos(x) - e^x \sin(x)</m>; <m> f''(x) = -2 e^x \sin(x)</m>; and <m> f'''(x)=-2e^x(\sin (x) + \cos (x))</m>. It follows that <m> P_2(x) = 1 + x</m> and <m> R_2(x) = f''''(\xi) \frac {x^3}{3!}</m>.</p>
<p>
a) Error bound using the remainder estimating <m> f(0.5)</m> using <m> R_2(0.5)</m> translates into an upper bound of <m> f'''(x)</m> over the interval of <m>(0,0.5)</m>. The upper bound of <m>\sin (x) + \cos (x)</m> is <m> \sqrt{2}</m> and so the upper bound for <m> R_2(0.5) = 2\sqrt{2}*e^{0.5}\frac {0.5^3}{3!} \approx 0.0972</m>. Note that the actual absolute error is <m> \vert f(0.5) - P_2(0.5)\vert \approx  0.0531</m>.</p>

<p> b) Repeat the bound process for the interval <m> [0,1]</m>, which means that <m> R_2(0.5) = 2e^{1}\frac {1^3}{3!} \approx 0.9061</m>.  Note the actual absolute error is  <m> \vert f(1) - P_2(1)\vert \approx  0.5313</m>.</p>

<p> c) Estimate the integral of <m> \int_0^1 f(x) \  dx</m> by calculating <m> \int_0^1 P_2(x) \ dx</m>, which in this case  <m> \int_0^1 P_2(x) \ dx = 1.5</m></p>

<p> d) Find an upper bound of error by calculating <m> \int_0^1\vert R_2(x) \vert \ dx </m>. An estimate to use in this case is that <m> \vert R_2(x) \vert \le 2e^1\frac {x^3}6</m>, which results in <m> \int_0^1  2e^1\frac {x^3}6 \ dx \approx  0.2265 </m>. The actual error of <m> \biggr \vert  \int_0^1 f(x)-P_2(x) \ dx \biggr \vert \approx 0.1220</m> </p>
</solution>
</exercise> 

<exercise number=" ">
    <title>Question 2.A</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            In another book, one can find the expression for the Taylor's Polynomial as:
            <m>T_{n}(x) = \sum_{j=0}^{n} \biggr ( \frac{f^{(j)}(x)}{j!}(x-x_{0})^{j} \biggr )</m>
            Is the expression above equivalent to the definition ([LB16]) in the book given in section 2? Please explain your answer.
        </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
        <p> This solution has a typographical error. Notice the evaluation of <m> f^{(j)}(x)</m> is at <m>x</m> when it should be at <m>x_0</m>. Hence, it follows that <m>T_n(x) = \sum_{j=0}^n \biggr ( \frac {f^{(j)}(x_0)}{j!}(x-x_0)^j \biggr )</m> is an equivalent representation of the Taylor Polynomial of degree n.
            </p>
    </solution>
</exercise>

<exercise number=" ">
    <title>Question 2.B</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            In a Taylor Polynomial approximation, the actual function is not known and that certainly means that <m>f^{(n)}(x)</m> is not known. However, suppose it can be determined that <m>\vert f^{(n)}(x)\vert</m> is decreasing over the interval that contains <m>x_{0}</m> and <m>x</m>. Explain how the status of a decreasing <m>\vert f^{(n)}(x)\vert</m> would help in coming up with an error bound estimate for a Taylor's Polynomial of degree <m>n-1</m>.
        </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
        <p>  The remainder term for a polynomial of degree n-1 is given as: <m> R(x) = \frac {f^n(\xi)}{n!}(x-x_0)^n</m>. If <m> \vert f^n(x) \vert</m> is decreasing then that means the largest value of <m> \vert f^n(x) \vert</m>  is on the left boundary of the interval. In the case <m> x_0 \lt x</m> this means <m>\vert f^n(x_0)\vert \ge \vert f^n(x) \vert</m> for all <m>x</m> in the interval  and the error is bounded by: <m>\vert \frac {f^n(x_0)}{n!}(x-x_0)^n \vert</m>. 
            </p>
    </solution>
</exercise>

<exercise number=" ">
    <title>Question 2.C</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            In the remainder term, <m>R_{n}(x) = \frac{f^{(n+1)}(\xi)}{(n+1)!}(x-x_{0})^{n+1}</m>, the theorem guarantees that <m>\xi</m> is in between <m>x</m> and <m>x_{0}</m>. Does the theorem apply if <m>x>x_{0}</m>, or <m>x&#x3C;x_{0}</m>, or both cases? Please explain your answer.
        </p>
    </statement>
</exercise>

<exercise number=" ">
    <title>Question 2.D</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            The remainder term, <m>R_{n}(x)</m> is often called the error term since it contains the error between <m>f(x)</m> and <m>T_{n}(x)</m>. Review the mathematical expression of <m>R_{n}(x)</m> and describe the elements that drive convergence of <m>T_{n}(x)</m> to <m>f(x)</m> (there should be three different features to discuss).
        </p>
    </statement>
</exercise>

