<exercise number=" ">
    <title>Question 3.A</title>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            Use Matlab to numerically estimate the rate of convergence of the sequence:
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>a) <m>\frac{n^{2}}{1+n^{2}}\rightarrow 1</m></p>
        </statement>
        <solution><p> This can be estimated by a calculator or by Matlab. The use of Matlab:</p>
<program><input>
>> p = @(x) x.^2./(1+x.^2);
>> ps = 1;
>> n=10000;
>> numerator = log(abs((p(n+2)-ps)/(p(n+1)-ps)));
>> denominator = log(abs((p(n+1)-ps)/(p(n)-ps)));
>> alpha = numerator/denominator
alpha = 0.9999
</input></program>
<p>Alpha converging to 1 means the order of convergence is linear (<m> alpha =1</m>).</p>
        </solution>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>b) <m>\frac{n!}{n^{n}}\rightarrow 0</m></p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>c) <m>(1 + \frac{1}{n})^{n} \rightarrow e</m></p>
        </statement>
        <solution><p>In MATLAB we can use the estimate:</p><program><input>
>> p = @(x) x.^2./(1+x.^2);
>> ps = exp(1);
>> n=10000;
>> numerator = log(abs((p(n+2)-ps)/(p(n+1)-ps)));
>> denominator = log(abs((p(n+1)-ps)/(p(n)-ps)));
>> alpha = numerator/denominator
</input></program>
<p><m>alpha \approx 0.9996</m></p>

        </solution>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>d) <m>\frac{1 + \cos (5n)}{n^{3} + 1}\rightarrow 0</m></p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>e) <m>\lim_{h \rightarrow 0}\frac{h^{2} + \cos (h) - e^{h}}{h} = -1</m>.</p>
        </statement>
    </task>
</exercise>

