<exercise number=" ">
    <title>Question 4.D</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Consider example 5.13 from [JR13] where radiation heat flux for a boiler tube is calculated from
            <md>
                <mrow>q = \epsilon \sigma (T^{4}_{F} - T^{4}_{S}),</mrow>
            </md>
            where <m>\epsilon</m> is emissivity of the surface of the boiler with <m>\epsilon = 0.9</m>, <m>\sigma</m> is the Stefan-Boltzmann constant with <m>\sigma = 1.355 \times 10^{-12}\ \frac{\text{cal}}{s\cdot\text{cm}^{2}\cdot\text{K}^{4}}</m>, <m>T_{F} = 3300 \ ^{o}\text{F}</m> is the furnace temperature, and <m>T_{S} = 3000 \ ^{o}\text{F}</m> is the temperature of the surface of the boiler tube. Use Matlab and the given information to calculate q.
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Multiple choice: 45.8436, 45.8436 <m>\frac{\text{cal}}{s\cdot\text{cm}^{2}}</m>, 45.8436 cal, 6.5679, 6.5679 <m>\frac{\text{cal}}{s\cdot\text{cm}^{2}}</m>, or 6.5679 cal.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </statement>
</exercise>

<p>
    Notes on working your calculator:
    <ul>
        <li><p>Keep all your calculations in your calculator with as much storage as possible and round ONLY at the end.</p></li>
        <li><p>Make sure you use radian measure for trigonometry functions, unless otherwise directed.</p></li>
        <li><p><alert>Make sure units of measure match</alert>.</p></li>
        <li><p>Be sure to list the units for anything that has a specific unit of measure.</p></li>
        <li><p>Nearest 0.001 is a specific value.</p></li>
        <li><p>If in doubt list as many digits as possible in final answer.</p></li>
    </ul>
</p>

