<exercise number=" ">
    <title>Question 5.A</title>
    <task>
        <p>
            Consider the case of a temperature profile of a rod given by <m>T(x) = T_{0} + qx^{2}</m>, where <m>T_{0} = 20^{O}</m> C, <m>q=5</m>, and <m>0\le x \le 1</m>. To match up units <m>q</m> has units of Celsius per cm <m>^{2}</m> and x is in measured in centimeters. Generate a plot of x vs. T(x) using Matlab.
        </p>
        <solution>
            <p>
                There are several ways to do this, but one example:
<program><input>
>> x = 0:0.01:1;
>> T0 = 20; q=5;
>> T = T0 + q*x.^2;
>> plot(x,T); xlabel('x (cm)'); 
>> ylabel('T (^OC)'); title('Plot of temp in rod in Celsius');
</input></program>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement><p>a) What step size did you think worked best for this curve?</p></statement>
        <solution><p>There isn't an object answer to this question. The example used 0.01, but one could use larger or smaller step size to generate results.</p></solution>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement><p>b) How small can you make the step size?</p></statement>
        <solution><p>There are two constraints to consider. If you select a step size that is too small then the size of x could run out of memory. In terms of machine arithmetic, we can't pick a step size smaller than machine epsilon.</p></solution>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement><p>c) If <m>x</m> was not given a bound in the problem then would there be any restrictions for <m>x</m> in terms of what Matlab can handle?</p></statement>
        <solution><p>There are two constraints to consider. In machine arithmetic, x could run into overflow. It is more likely T would encounter overflow since it involves the square of x. In terms of the physical application, there are limits to how long a rod can be made and how far heat could distribute across a long rod. </p></solution>
    </task>
</exercise>

<exercise number=" ">
    <title>Question 5.B</title>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            Consider the discrete model for Newton's Law of Cooling
            <md>
                <mrow>T_{n+1}= T_{n} + h \cdot (-k(T_{n} - T_{\text{env}})),</mrow>
            </md>
            where <m>T_{n}</m> is the temperature at step n, <m>T_{\text{env}}</m> is the ambient temperature, <m>k</m> is a cooling constant, and <m>h</m> is the step size between steps in time. Consider the case when <m>T_{0} = 90</m>, <m>k = 0.1</m>, <m>h=1</m>, and <m>T_{\text{env}}=25</m>. Write a Matlab program that would do 30 iterations of this model in a loop.
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
        <statement><p>a) What would help make this more accurate?</p></statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement><p>b) What should we expect to happen as time gets larger (run the loop for more iterations)?</p></statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement><p>c) Would this model apply if we had an example where <m>T_{\text{env}}</m> is larger than <m>T_{0}</m>?</p></statement>
    </task>
</exercise>

<exercise number=" ">
    <title>Question 5.C</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            The sample program of pracs3.m implements the computation of the Maclaurin Polynomial for the function <m>f(x) = x^{4}-2x^{2}+x-3</m>. Adjust this program to calculate the Maclaurin Polynomial for <m>g(x)=\frac{1}{3} x^{5} - x^{3}+\frac{1}{2} x^{2} + x +1</m>.
        </p>
    </statement>
</exercise>

<exercise number=" ">
    <title>Question 5.D</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            The sample program of pracs3.m implements the computation of the Maclaurin Polynomial for the function <m>f(x) = x^{4}-2x^{2}+x-3</m>. Adjust this program to calculate the Maclaurin Polynomial for <m>h(x) = \cos (x)</m>.
        </p>
    </statement>
</exercise>

<exercise number=" ">
    <title>Question 5.E</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            What are the rules of thumb discussed in class that can increase error due to machine round off?
        </p>
    </statement>
</exercise>

</exercises>

